## Problem 1 — the onion, with a guard

Edit `ex1/solution.ts`. Export

```ts
type Middleware<C> = (ctx: C, next: () => Promise<void>) => unknown;
function compose<C>(middlewares: ReadonlyArray<Middleware<C>>):
  (ctx: C, done?: () => unknown) => Promise<void>;
```

The composed function runs the middlewares in order, each receiving a `next`
that resolves only when everything below it has settled, so code after
`await next()` runs on the way back out. Specifically:

- an empty list resolves after calling `done` (default: a no-op);
- a middleware that returns a non-promise still composes;
- a middleware that throws synchronously produces a rejected promise, not a
  thrown error;
- a rejection anywhere below is catchable with `try`/`catch` around
  `await next()` in any middleware above;
- calling `next()` twice in one middleware rejects with an `Error` whose
  message includes `next() called multiple times` — and it must reject the
  second call, not corrupt the first;
- a middleware that never calls `next` ends the run: nothing below it, and not
  `done`, executes.

