exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

The recursion is the mechanism. `dispatch(i)` runs middleware `i` and hands it
a `next` that is nothing more than `() => dispatch(i + 1)`. Because that call
returns a promise, awaiting it suspends the current frame until every deeper
frame has settled — the "onion" is just a JavaScript call stack with awaits in
it, not a data structure.

## Hint 2

Detect the double call with a high-water mark rather than a per-frame flag:

```ts
let index = -1;
const dispatch = (i: number): Promise<void> => {
  if (i <= index) return Promise.reject(new Error("next() called multiple times"));
  index = i;
  ...
};
```

A single shared number catches both the obvious case (calling `next()` twice in
a row) and the sneaky one (stashing `next` and calling it later, after the
stack has moved on).

## Hint 3

Two wrappers make the contract uniform. `Promise.resolve(fn(ctx, next))` lets a
synchronous middleware participate. A `try`/`catch` around the *call itself*
turns a synchronous `throw` into a rejection:

```ts
try {
  return Promise.resolve(fn(ctx, () => dispatch(i + 1))).then(() => undefined);
} catch (error) {
  return Promise.reject(error);
}
```

Without that `catch`, a middleware that throws before its first `await` escapes
the promise chain entirely and becomes an uncaught exception — in a server,
that is a crashed process rather than a 500.

