## Problem 3 — a router that knows the difference between 404 and 405

Edit `ex3/solution.ts`. Export `createRouter()` returning

```ts
{
  add(method: string, pattern: string, handler: H): void;
  find(method: string, pathname: string): Result;
}

type Result =
  | { kind: "found"; handler: H; params: Record<string, string>; head: boolean }
  | { kind: "method"; allow: string[] }
  | { kind: "missing" };
```

Use your `matchPath` from ex2 (copy it in).

- Methods are compared case-insensitively and stored uppercased.
- `kind: "missing"` when no registered pattern matches the path at all.
- `kind: "method"` when some pattern matched but none with this method;
  `allow` is the sorted, de-duplicated list of methods registered on the
  matching patterns, **plus `HEAD` whenever `GET` is among them**.
- `HEAD` falls back to a `GET` route: `find("HEAD", "/users")` with only a
  `GET /users` registered returns that handler with `head: true`. A `HEAD`
  route registered explicitly wins over the fallback and reports `head: false`.
- Registering the same method and pattern twice is an `Error` — silent
  shadowing is how two files each think they own an endpoint.

