## Problem 4 — a body the client can branch on

Edit `ex4/solution.ts`. Export `problem(init)` returning
`{ status, headers, body }`, where `body` is the serialised RFC 9457 document
and `headers` carries `content-type: application/problem+json; charset=utf-8`
and an exact `content-length`.

```ts
interface ProblemInit {
  type?: string;            // default "about:blank"
  title: string;
  status: number;
  detail?: string;
  instance?: string;
  errors?: Issue[];
  extensions?: Record<string, unknown>;
}
```

Rules:

- key order in the serialised document is `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`,
  `instance`, then `errors`, then extensions in their own insertion order —
  JSON objects are unordered by spec and ordered in practice, and a stable
  order makes diffs and snapshot tests readable;
- fields that are `undefined` are omitted entirely, never serialised as
  `null`;
- `status` must be an integer in 400–599 or it is a `TypeError`;
- `type` must be `about:blank` or an absolute URI (it has a scheme) — a
  relative string is a `TypeError`, because a `type` is an identifier, not a
  link to your docs site;
- an extension whose key collides with a core field is a `TypeError` — silent
  overwriting of `status` in an error body is a debugging nightmare;
- `errors` is omitted when empty rather than serialised as `[]`.

